"""Original extended model responses and rehearsal tasks."""
BRIDGE_MODEL='''The report invites readers to see the bridge’s restoration as useful but incomplete. Its factual opening establishes reopening after a defined repair period, and the engineer’s attributed statement that the structure is ready for use gives that event technical legitimacy. The mayor’s description of a connection restored then broadens structural readiness into a celebratory social claim.

The report qualifies that claim by including different users. The shop owner’s shortened journey supplies a concrete benefit, supporting the idea that reopening matters beyond the ceremony. However, the wheelchair user’s question about the uneven approach shifts the measure of success from the deck to the whole route. Placing this concern after the official praise allows readers to recognise that a connection may be restored for some people while remaining difficult for others. The mayor’s reference to a separate project does not resolve the immediate concern.

The closing image of pedestrians crossing between temporary warning signs leaves the account visually unfinished after the ribbon has gone. Rather than announcing that the event is either a success or a failure, this sequence encourages readers to test official celebration against practical conditions. The missing subcontractor information also limits scrutiny of the repair process, although its absence does not prove wrongdoing. Through attribution, juxtaposition and a qualified ending, the report positions readers to value the reopened bridge while questioning how fully the broader promise of access has been met.'''
POSTER_A_MODEL='''The poster represents a public library as an invitation to personal possibility. A bright doorway is centred within a dark field, making the contrast between outside and inside its dominant visual relationship. Light extends towards a small figure rather than remaining confined within the building. This arrangement can suggest that the service reaches towards a prospective user, whose individual presence gives access a human scale.

The headline A place for your next chapter links the literal activity of reading with a figurative future. Your directly addresses a viewer, while next chapter leaves the nature of that future open: study, language learning or another change could all fit the invitation. The library’s description as a shared public space supports this broad understanding of belonging rather than a narrowly commercial one.

The contrasting opening-hours strip gives the welcoming image a practical condition. Thursday, the late session and free entry tell the viewer when and on what terms the invitation can be acted upon. This information matters because an attractive doorway alone could offer only an atmosphere. Image, headline and usable details therefore work together to make welcome both metaphorical and actionable. Nevertheless, the poster cannot establish that every user’s actual access needs are met. Its choices invite an inclusive interpretation; a complete judgement about the real service would require verified information beyond this fictional design.'''
POSTER_B_MODEL='''The alternative poster foregrounds a public library’s measurable output rather than the experience of an individual user. Its large annual total dominates the centre, drawing attention before the smaller doorway and opening-hours line. The row of identical book icons reinforces this numerical emphasis by presenting repeated transactions as the service’s most visible activity.

Measure what moves gives that arrangement an argumentative direction. The imperative asks the viewer to judge the library through movement which can be counted. Naming the figure as annual loan transactions limits what the number actually measures, but the poster’s hierarchy may invite a broader inference that high transaction volume establishes value. That inference should be questioned: the design supplies no information about study, assistance or users who enter without borrowing.

The small doorway remains present, yet it receives little visual priority. Similarly, the late-session information sits at the lower edge rather than becoming part of a prominent invitation. This makes practical access subordinate to recorded output. The poster need not claim that people are unimportant in order to make them less visible; its selection and scale perform that emphasis. A critical response can recognise transactions as a legitimate measure while rejecting the idea that this single total captures every public benefit. The fictional design therefore illustrates how salience and language together can narrow a service’s implied purpose without changing its name or its stated opening hours.'''
ESSAY_MODEL='''The names on the wall challenges the assumption that a place’s value can be adequately described through its economic use. Its narrator remembers a repair shop not to demand that every old building remain, but to ask how necessary change can acknowledge the lives it replaces. Through selected physical details, a contested word and a practical concession, the essay constructs a qualified perspective on progress.

The opening decision to photograph the wall rather than the sign gives the narrator’s attention a clear direction. A sign would identify the business publicly; the wall preserves traces of what happened inside. Outlines left by spanners suggest repeated work and an organised sharing of tools, while names and pencil marks make earlier occupants visible without presenting their full histories. These details make the room more than floor area. They establish a social and educational use which the later development vocabulary will struggle to recognise. The photograph is significant because it selects evidence of people’s activity, not simply an attractive surface from an old building.

The essay then tests the brochure’s description of the site as underused. The narrator provisionally accepts the word when use is measured as floor area sold. This concession matters: the argument does not depend on proving the brochure’s description false under every criterion. Calling it an odd word for a room where asking for help was learned introduces another measure. The comparison reveals that a description can be technically defensible within an economic framework while remaining inadequate to a social experience. The narrator challenges what the word counts rather than merely replacing it with a more flattering adjective. A reader is therefore invited to examine the values inside apparently neutral language about development.

The practical qualification in the final movement prevents this alternative value from becoming an absolute preservation claim. A leaking roof and unsafe stairs are material problems. Their inclusion acknowledges that affection and memory cannot make the premises suitable for continuing use. The narrator explicitly rejects wanting every old building preserved, separating the argument from a familiar opposition between progress and nostalgia. This distinction makes the perspective more defensible: it asks a description of change to admit a loss without treating that admission as resistance to improvement. The essay’s complexity lies in holding recognition and repair within the same argument, not in avoiding a judgement about either.

The ending returns to the photograph and states its limited function. It does not repair the roof; it keeps the names legible. The contrast gives preservation a different object. What can be retained is an acknowledgement of learning and mutual help, even if the structure changes. Returning to the opening wall also gives the reflective argument coherence. Its central image has developed from an instinctive act of memory into a considered account of what memory can and cannot do. The ending remains modest rather than pretending to solve the development’s cost, design or relocation decisions.

These choices encourage a reader to understand progress as a judgement made through selected values. Economic efficiency remains relevant, and structural defects remain real, but neither exhausts the meaning of the place. The essay is effective as a challenge to an incomplete vocabulary because it supplies an alternative criterion and tests its own limits. It does not establish that redevelopment must be stopped or that every reader should share the narrator’s attachment. Instead, it makes a narrower and more sustained case: change can be necessary without descriptions of that change making earlier lives disappear.'''
COMPOSE_MODEL='''Some things could not be packed.

I discovered this after arranging the shirts in two careful stacks. The suitcase accepted the shirts, the spare charger, the shoes wrapped in yesterday’s newspaper. Each item made the room look more manageable. I could turn a life into a list if I did not look up.

My brother stood in the doorway with the broken compass. The needle still moved, but it no longer agreed with anything. We had bought it at a market and used it for expeditions which never went beyond the reserve behind our street.

“You forgot this,” he said.

I had not forgotten it. I had left it in the desk drawer under a receipt, where an object could wait without making a demand. There would be no need for a compass in the city. There were maps on phones and signs above platforms. I had prepared that answer too.

“It doesn’t work,” I said.

He put it on the folded shirts. The needle swung towards the metal zip and stayed there.

Outside, our father was checking the tyres of a car which had been serviced the day before. The sound of the pump came through the open window in short, regular bursts. We had all found practical tasks. No one had asked what would happen to the desk when I left.

My brother picked up a loose button from the floor. He rolled it between his fingers, studying it as if it might belong to something important. I remembered teaching him how to follow the creek without stepping on the slippery stones. He had been certain the compass made us explorers. I had been certain knowing the way made me responsible for him.

“You can keep it,” I said.

“I already know where the reserve is.”

It was not an accusation. That made it harder to answer. I had thought giving him the compass would preserve my part in the story: the older person leaving a tool and some useful knowledge behind. His reply placed us both in the present. He could find his own way to the creek. I was the one going somewhere I did not know.

The pump stopped. Our father called that there was still time for breakfast, using the voice he used when a journey had an ordinary timetable.

I took the compass off the shirts and held it level. The needle trembled, pointed towards the zip, then drifted away. My brother watched without offering a correction.

“Will you show me the new room when you get there?” he asked.

I said I would. It was a small request, something a phone could carry. The relief of having an answer almost made me put the compass back in the drawer. Instead I opened the outside pocket of the suitcase. It fitted beside the ticket.

At breakfast, my brother described a path through the reserve which I had never used. He spoke with the confidence I remembered having. I listened until he reached the bridge, then asked which bank the path followed after rain.

In the car, the compass stayed in the pocket. It could not direct us to the city. It could not keep my brother small or make my leaving temporary. When the house disappeared behind the bend, I pressed my hand against the case and felt its round edge through the fabric.

There was room for it. That was different from knowing what it could carry.'''
